WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:06.660
[music]

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:08.820
Hi, I'm Christopher Crockett,

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:11.220
and I'm the astronomy writer at Science News.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:15.820
In 2017, we'll be learning about new things in our own

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:17.900
solar system to the center of the galaxy

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:20.320
out to galaxies billions of light-years away.

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:24.420
One of the things I'm looking forward to in 2017 is

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:27.980
astronomers figuring out where fast radio bursts

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:28.900
are coming from.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:33.840
These are these really big blasts of radio waves

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.480
that appear to be coming from other galaxies,

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.480
but we have no idea what's causing them.

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:41.340
Astronomers are getting really close to

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.680
narrowing down where these things are coming from

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:46.060
by detecting them at one telescope

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:48.380
and then quickly catching them with another telescope.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:50.600
And I've heard some rumors that there might be some

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.580
really cool discoveries and announcements

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:53.980
coming up pretty soon.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:56.560
A bit closer to home, there's a project

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:58.840
called the Even Horizon Telescope

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:00.960
that's been running for about a decade.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.040
What they're doing is linking together radio telescopes

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:08.140
around the world to create one giant virtual telescope

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:09.840
that's about the size of the Earth,

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:11.560
and what they're hoping to do is

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.770
actually see the supermassive black hole

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:16.850
that's about 28,000 light-years away

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:18.180
in the center of the galaxy.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:20.200
And we've never been able to actually see a black hole

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:22.280
before because, well, because they're black.

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:23.900
They don't give off any light,

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:25.680
but what they're looking for is actually

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:29.680
light that's being bent around it by its immense gravity.

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:32.220
It's a phenomenally difficult thing to do.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:35.080
It's like trying to see an orange

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:36.320
on the surface of the moon.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:39.060
The Juno mission is at Jupiter,

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:41.700
and it's been there since July.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.300
But now it's going to really start doing

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:46.240
its regular science, mapping the interior of Jupiter,

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:48.920
getting a 3-D picture of what the biggest planet

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:50.980
in our solar system is like on the inside --

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:52.820
looking for how much water there is,

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:54.360
whether or not there's a core.

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:57.200
That will tell us a lot about how Jupiter formed,

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:58.900
and that can tell us a lot about

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:00.819
how the rest of the solar system formed

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:03.040
and solar systems throughout the galaxy.

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:05.280
But I think one of the coolest things

00:02:05.289 --> 00:02:06.609
going on in the solar system this year

00:02:06.609 --> 00:02:09.229
is the end of the Cassini mission at Saturn.

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:12.180
After almost 20 years in space

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:14.620
and over a decade at Saturn,

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:16.360
the mission is coming to an end.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:17.860
The spacecraft is going to start going

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:19.200
where it's never gone before.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.120
It's going to start repeatedly diving

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:22.920
between the rings and the planet.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:23.980
As they fly through the rings,

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:25.580
they're gonna spin the spacecraft,

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:28.480
so that the big radio dish is facing forward

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:30.640
They're using that as like a deflector shield,

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.440
and so if anything from the rings hits it,

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:33.780
it'll just bounce off the antenna

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:35.860
and protect the instruments behind it.

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.020
So that'll allow them to get really close to Saturn,

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:39.880
see details they've never seen before.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:41.480
They'll actually be able to figure out

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:43.200
just how massive the rings are

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:44.880
which might tell them where they came from.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:47.980
And then in September, the mission is going to end

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:50.900
by deflecting and diving into the atmosphere,

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:53.380
and spacecraft will plunge to its death,

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:55.680
making measurements all the way down.

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:57.400
The reason they're ending it that way

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:01.800
is actually to protect the icy moons around Saturn --

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.540
moons that we think have

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:05.760
saltwater oceans beneath them.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:08.281
Cassini may still carry some sort of

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:11.310
biological contamination on it,

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:13.320
and they don't want to remotely risk

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:17.110
our biology getting into the oceans of Saturn's moons.

00:03:17.110 --> 00:03:18.280
So they're just going to toss the

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:19.860
spacecraft away into the planet.

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:22.460
It's always a really cool year

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:23.640
to be writing about astronomy.

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:25.480
We're always discovering new things,

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:28.120
and I'm sure 2017 will be no different.

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:32.600
[music]

